              "audio": "media/audio/set12/l1-q25-28.mp3",
              "scriptOrigin": "script-docx",
              "script": "So today, I want to talk about William Grant Still, an American composer sometimes called the \"Dean of African American Composers.\" Still grew up in Little Rock, Arkansas. His father, who was a local bandmaster, passed away when Still was just an infant. But his stepfather really nurtured his love of music - he bought recordings of classical operas for the boy and took him to live performances regularly. That early exposure made a huge impression.\nStill eventually studied at the Oberlin Conservatory of Music, where he learned composition and theory rooted in the European classical tradition. He also studied privately with the avant-garde French composer Edgard Varese. So he had this really interesting blend of influences - traditional European form on one hand, and experimental modern techniques on the other.\nNow, his most celebrated work is the \"Afro-American Symphony,\" completed in 1931. This piece is warm and lyrical. It draws heavily on blues melodies and spirituals, and there's a beautiful balance between those folk elements and the formal structure of a classical symphony. It really captures the emotional landscape of everyday African American life.\nBut Still also explored more dramatic territory. His ballet \"Sahdji,\" set in Africa, was composed after extensive study of African musical traditions. It uses layered percussion and choral singing to depict a ceremonial scene. Critics regarded it as a powerful expression of cultural heritage and identity."
