              "audio": "media/audio/set12/l1-q29-32.mp3",
              "scriptOrigin": "script-docx",
              "script": "Alright, let's talk about something fascinating - how very young children understand numbers. This is a topic that has kept researchers busy for decades, because it tells us so much about how the human mind develops.\nSo, one classic experiment is the dot comparison task. Researchers showed toddlers, around three years old, two cards. One card had, say, five dots, and the other had fifteen. And what they found was that even before these children could count reliably, they could point to the card with more dots. In other words, they had an intuitive sense of quantity - a basic number sense - without any formal instruction.\nOK, so a second experiment took a different approach. This one used what we call the violation of expectation method, with even younger babies, around five months old. Here's how it worked. Babies watched as one toy was placed on a small stage. Then a screen went up, and a second toy was added behind it. When the screen dropped, sometimes it revealed two toys, which is correct, and sometimes it showed just one. The babies stared significantly longer at the unexpected result - the single toy - which suggests they expected one plus one to equal two.\nSo basically, babies are intuitive mathematicians. They seem to grasp basic quantities from very early on. Now, the question is - does this early number sense connect to other kinds of abstract reasoning? Let's look at some research exploring that connection."
