              "audio": "media/audio/set12/l2-q08-11.mp3",
              "scriptOrigin": "script-docx",
              "script": "So, when people hear the words \"public transit,\" they often think about crowded buses, delays, noise - basically all the negative stuff. And sure, poorly managed systems can contribute to pollution and congestion. However, when cities invest wisely in their transit networks, the results can actually be surprisingly positive for the environment.\nTake Bogota, Colombia, for example. In the early 2000s, the city built a bus rapid transit system with dedicated lanes and efficient stations. The result? Many residents who previously drove chose to ride the bus instead. This cut down on traffic congestion and noticeably improved air quality in the city center.\nNow, contrast that with car-dependent suburban areas. When neighborhoods are spread out, everyone needs a private vehicle. That means more fuel consumption, more roads to build and maintain, and infrastructure costs stretched across a huge area. So, paradoxically, those quiet suburban neighborhoods can actually have a larger environmental footprint per person than a dense city with well-designed transit.\nAnd here's another interesting case - Seoul has been converting elevated highways into green parks and pedestrian zones. This freed up space for community gardens where residents grow food locally. As a result, these neighborhoods have reduced their dependence on produce shipped in from distant farms.\nSo the takeaway is this: thoughtful investment in public transit and urban redesign can make dense cities significantly greener than low-density suburbs."
